snatch","H4941":"judgement","H2820":"to withhold","H7626":"staff","H157":"lover","H7836":"to seek","H7648":"satiety","H990":"abdomen","H2637":"to lack"},"ccc":{"1":[2216]},"cccp":{"2216":"__P7U#"},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Doctrine. Or he gives proof of his good education, (Calmet) and excites even his father to advance in piety. (Ven. Bede) — Septuagint, “is obedient to his father; but the disobedient son is in destruction.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Mouth. In reward of his good speeches. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “of justice the good man eateth; but the souls of the lawless perish before the time,” chap. xii. 13."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Hath. Hebrew, “who openeth his lips inconsiderately, is lost, Judges xi. 35. (Haydock)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Willeth not. Hebrew, “hath not.” He only desires riches, or to be converted, and will not labour, chap. xxi. 25., and Ecclesiasticus v. 8."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Confounded. The detractor is like swine, stirring up dirt. (St. Chrysostom, 32. ad Pop.) — The liar is not believed, even when he speaks the truth. (Aristotle)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Sinner. Symmachus, “draweth on sin.” Virtue is the best safeguard."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Riches. Such was St. Paul, 2 Corinthians vi. 10. Some affect to be rich, while others are never satisfied. Semper avarus eget. Lazarus was very rich in God’s sight."],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Reprehension. Or is not able to defend himself, like the rich. (Calmet) — He is not exposed so much to great revolutions. (Bayn.)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Out. They are hated as well as their offspring. (Calmet)"],["haydock",10,null,"augustine","Contentions. As none will yield. (Menochius) — Hebrew, “only by pride cometh contention.” (Protestants) — “Pride is the mother of all sects.” (St. Augustine)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Haste. Hebrew, “by vanity,” and injustice. Those who become rich on a sudden fall under suspicion, as a Roman objected to Sylla, who had inherited nothing. (Plut.[Plutarch?]) — By little. Hebrew, “he that gathereth by labour, (Septuagint, piety) shall increase.” (Protestants) (Haydock)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Hope. Septuagint, “(the just shews mercy and lends) better is he who begins heartily to assist, than he who promises and leads to hope. For a bad (Grabe substitutes good) desire is a tree of life.” — Soul. Protestants, “maketh the heart sick.” (Haydock) — The pain increases in proportion to our eager desire. Calvin maintains, that the souls of the blessed are not yet in heaven, but hope: and of course he would establish a sort of purgatory for them. (Haydock)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Come. To defend what he has asserted, or to pass for a liar. Hebrew, “shall be destroyed by it.” (Mont.[Montanus?]) (Haydock) — Those who despise God’s order shall perish. — Deceitful, &c. This is not in Hebrew, nor in some of the Latin editions. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “to the deceitful son nothing shall be good. But the ways of the wise servant shall prosper, and his paths shall be made straight,” chap. xiv. 15. (Haydock)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","That. Septuagint, “but the fool is slain in the snare.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Grace. God assists those who strive to be well instructed. (Calmet)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Of the. Hebrew, “a wicked ambassador.” A king generally chooses people like himself. (Menochius) — Septuagint have read melec. “A rash king shall,” &c."],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","To him. Hebrew, “destroy discipline,” so that the most wretched are often noted for immorality. Septuagint, “instruction takes away poverty,” as “the whole earth supports the man that has a trade,” technion, according to the Greek proverb. (Calmet)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","That is. Septuagint, “of the pious, but the works of the impious are far from knowledge.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Become. Septuagint, “be known.” A person’s disposition may be seen by the company which he frequents."],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Heirs. This was more observable under the old law: yet we often see the distress to which the unjust are exposed. De male quæsitis non gaudet tertius hæres. (Calmet)"],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Fathers. Heirs often lose their property by their misconduct. Hebrew and (Haydock) Chaldean read, “of the poor,” who till their land better than those who have too large farms. (Menochius) — Nature requires but little. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “the just shall spend many years in affluence: but the unjust are cut off at once.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Betimes. God has always treated his friends in this manner, to preserve them from sin, or to increase their reward. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"augustine":["Augustine of Hippo",1,1,430,"https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/#augustine"]}}